<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1/policyconfig.dtd">
<!--
  VELTRIX background updater — privileged apply action.

  What this authorises: running /usr/bin/veltrix-update-helper (the
  apply half of the background updater) as root, WITHOUT a password,
  for the active local session. The helper is deliberately narrow: it
  can ONLY install the release the unprivileged downloader staged, and
  only after re-verifying that tarball's SHA-256 against the pinned
  GitHub API itself. It takes no URL, no path, no version and no hash
  from its caller, it cannot downgrade, and there is no credential
  anywhere for a compromised updater to steal — no sudo password is
  saved, in any file, keyring or environment variable, not even briefly.
  That is why this action needs no password prompt: there is no secret
  to protect, and the action itself can only install a release GitHub
  already published.

  Why polkit + pkexec, and not a D-Bus system service: the existing
  precedent in this tree is the Content Safety action
  (org.veltrix.content-safety.*), which already authorises a narrow
  root helper through pkexec with the same allow_active=yes shape.
  A D-Bus system service would add an always-on root daemon, a bus
  policy, and a second IPC surface for a job that runs once per
  update; pkexec spawns the helper on demand and exits. The polkit
  policy is the whole authorisation surface, and it is one file.

  allow_active=yes: the user sitting at the machine (the active local
  session) applies an update without a password — that is the point of
  the background updater (no terminal, no prompt, no yes/no).
  allow_any / allow_inactive=auth_admin: a remote or inactive session
  still needs an admin password, so an update cannot be triggered
  from anywhere but the machine itself.

  The org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path annotation pins the action to
  this one binary. pkexec refuses to run anything else under it.
-->
<policyconfig>
  <action id="org.veltrix.update.apply">
    <description>Install a verified VELTRIX update</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to install a VELTRIX update</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>yes</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/usr/bin/veltrix-update-helper</annotate>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.argv1">apply</annotate>
  </action>
</policyconfig>
